you'll get through those
first two things very quickly,

00:11:36.183 --> 00:11:38.764
those first many grade levels.

00:11:38.764 --> 00:11:41.559
I think if you're like a lot
of adults probably around

00:11:41.559 --> 00:11:44.104
you know, some of the
more advanced arithmetic

00:11:44.104 --> 00:11:47.864
with decimals and maybe some
exponents and negative numbers

00:11:47.864 --> 00:11:48.814
that's when you're going to start

00:11:48.814 --> 00:11:51.614
needing some significant
refreshers, that's going to be

00:11:51.614 --> 00:11:54.844
in fifth, sixth, seventh
grade math, and then a lot

00:11:54.844 --> 00:11:58.074
of adults I think need, need
help refreshing in the algebra.

00:11:58.074 --> 00:12:02.114
But if you did that, I would
say for 30 to 40 minutes a day

00:12:02.114 --> 00:12:04.944
say five days a week, I
think you're going to, first

00:12:04.944 --> 00:12:07.334
of all, be at your learning
edge in about a week or two.

00:12:07.334 --> 00:12:08.374
So you'll probably be

00:12:08.374 --> 00:12:10.754
at some of that, that early algebra stuff.

00:12:10.754 --> 00:12:11.993
And then you keep going master algebra

00:12:11.993 --> 00:12:16.346
master geometry, algebra two,

00:12:16.346 --> 00:12:21.244
master trigonometry, pre-calculus,
calculus, statistics.

00:12:21.244 --> 00:12:22.434
And if you want to be a math major

00:12:22.434 --> 00:12:24.664
I would say, then go on into
multi variable calculus.

00:12:24.664 --> 00:12:26.514
Now you can do all of
that on Khan Academy.

00:12:26.514 --> 00:12:27.888
Now that's going to take you some time

00:12:27.888 --> 00:12:29.204
depending on where you are.

00:12:29.204 --> 00:12:32.892
I think that might take
at least probably a year

00:12:32.892 --> 00:12:35.944
or so, depending on where
you are on your journey

00:12:35.944 --> 00:12:38.604
but we've had, I've gotten
letters from people in your boat

00:12:38.604 --> 00:12:40.084
who've done it in about that timeframe.

00:12:40.084 --> 00:12:41.706
Maybe it might take a little bit longer.

00:12:41.706 --> 00:12:44.567
And if you did that, you would
have an excellent foundation

00:12:44.567 --> 00:12:47.640
for becoming a math major in college.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:48.904
On top of that

00:12:48.904 --> 00:12:52.154
we have videos on differential
equations and linear algebra.

00:12:52.154 --> 00:12:53.753
We don't have exercises on those yet

00:12:53.753 --> 00:12:56.344
but I encourage you to
watch those pause the video

00:12:56.344 --> 00:12:58.721
try to work through it yourself
before I worked through it.

00:12:58.721 --> 00:12:59.584
And so, because

00:12:59.584 --> 00:13:04.584
if you have a good foundation
in algebra and calculus in a

00:13:04.604 --> 00:13:07.644
and especially more advanced
calculus and linear algebra

00:13:07.644 --> 00:13:11.024
I don't think the math
degree will be a problem, but

00:13:11.024 --> 00:13:13.884
you know, it's, it'll, it will
be a journey I would predict

00:13:13.884 --> 00:13:17.394
if you do this diligently,
you'll be ready to enroll

00:13:17.394 --> 00:13:21.164
at a local university
in about a year or two.

00:13:21.164 --> 00:13:24.114
And then you, you you'd be off
to the races on your degree.

00:13:25.174 --> 00:13:26.007
So late.

00:13:26.007 --> 00:13:27.177
Let's see.

00:13:27.177 --> 00:13:28.667
Next question.

00:13:31.754 --> 00:13:33.501
From YouTube Michael Walker asked,

00:13:33.501 --> 00:13:35.944
"How do you stay motivated Sal Khan?"

00:13:35.944 --> 00:13:37.174
I mean, sometimes it's hard to see

00:13:37.174 --> 00:13:39.224
the big picture when you
were an undergraduate

00:13:39.224 --> 00:13:40.057
and graduate student

00:13:40.057 --> 00:13:42.557
what did you do to keep on track?

00:13:44.774 --> 00:13:46.854
You know the thing that I remind myself

00:13:46.854 --> 00:13:48.004
is that life is short.

00:13:48.004 --> 00:13:50.804
And I, you know, I, maybe I
was an old soul even when I was

00:13:50.804 --> 00:13:51.724
in high school or when I was

00:13:51.724 --> 00:13:55.467
in college, but I always
felt that life is short.

00:13:56.348 --> 00:13:59.760
And the last thing that I
wanted to do is to kind of

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:01.810
you know, make, not make good use

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:05.664
of whatever precious moments
I had on this planet.

00:14:05.664 --> 00:14:08.104
And so for me, you know, I like everyone

00:14:08.104 --> 00:14:10.357
I have those days where things are not

00:14:10.357 --> 00:14:15.214
going my way and I'm not,
you know, let's call it,

00:14:15.214 --> 00:14:18.234
I don't have the best
self-esteem sometimes.

00:14:18.234 --> 00:14:19.471
But I just remind myself that, that

00:14:19.471 --> 00:14:22.514
you know, as much as I can,
I try to coach myself to say

00:14:22.514 --> 00:14:25.034
well, you know, that's
not going to help me.

00:14:25.034 --> 00:14:27.184
So let me do some small steps

00:14:27.184 --> 00:14:28.674
that might get me out of my funk.

00:14:28.674 --> 00:14:29.841
And I've talked about this before

00:14:29.841 --> 00:14:32.714
you know, I've made my bed religiously now

00:14:32.714 --> 00:14:35.884
for about 20 something years
because that's one small step.

00:14:35.884 --> 00:14:38.094
You're like, hey, at least I made my bed.

00:14:38.094 --> 00:14:40.744
And then, you know, every
morning I try to do some pushups.

00:14:40.744 --> 00:14:42.207
I try to do some, pull-ups.

00:14:43.406 --> 00:14:44.664
A new thing I've started over
the last two, three years

00:14:44.664 --> 00:14:46.184
is I take cold showers.

00:14:46.184 --> 00:14:47.144
All of these things

00:14:47.144 --> 00:14:50.884
have a way, I meditate
every day, 30 minutes

00:14:50.884 --> 00:14:54.714
and all of these things, they
do, they do two things for me.

00:14:54.714 --> 00:14:58.384
They give me wins no matter
what the day is going like,

00:14:58.384 --> 00:15:00.254
no matter what headwinds I'm facing

00:15:00.254 --> 00:15:01.600
or how I'm feeling about myself

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:05.144
or the world, I'm like, I
made my bed, I got in there.

00:15:05.144 --> 00:15:06.144
I took that cold shower

00:15:06.144 --> 00:15:08.274
which is like a reboot of your brain.

00:15:08.274 --> 00:15:09.884
I meditated which is also like

00:15:09.884 --> 00:15:11.614
another type of reboot of your brain.

00:15:11.614 --> 00:15:13.834
It kind of just opens it up.

00:15:13.834 --> 00:15:16.464
You, you start to really
still your mind, all those

00:15:16.464 --> 00:15:17.694
whatever negative thoughts

00:15:17.694 --> 00:15:20.624
they become a lot less
than, than they were.

00:15:20.624 --> 00:15:21.457
And you start, you know

00:15:21.457 --> 00:15:25.634
some positive thoughts start
to slowly, slowly come out.

00:15:25.634 --> 00:15:27.168
At least I find that when I meditate

00:15:27.168 --> 00:15:30.744
and then you do some exercise
gets your blood flowing

00:15:30.744 --> 00:15:31.888
and it makes you feel accomplished.

00:15:31.888 --> 00:15:32.721
And then, you know

00:15:32.721 --> 00:15:34.364
if you can do that, if you do
that sort of thing, at least

00:15:34.364 --> 00:15:37.614
for me, that set of things,
you know, if I can do that

00:15:37.614 --> 00:15:40.754
in the first hour or two of my day

00:15:40.754 --> 00:15:42.654
that I'm like, this is
going to be a good day.

00:15:42.654 --> 00:15:45.194
And you know, sometimes
it is sometimes it isn't.

00:15:45.194 --> 00:15:46.994
But I gotta say, especially the meditation

00:15:46.994 --> 00:15:49.414
even when something happens at work and it

00:15:49.414 --> 00:15:51.774
you know, it's disappointing
or something in the family

00:15:51.774 --> 00:15:54.074
and I'm like, ah, this
is going to be so hard.

00:15:55.314 --> 00:15:59.074
The meditation helps you
from getting overly attached

00:15:59.074 --> 00:16:02.144
to whatever that scenario
is, or even overly attached

00:16:02.144 --> 00:16:06.034
to your own ego or, or your own fears.

00:16:06.034 --> 00:16:07.354
And when you're not overly attached

00:16:07.354 --> 00:16:09.744
to those things, then
they don't feel as heavy.

00:16:09.744 --> 00:16:12.564
At least that's what I feel for myself.

00:16:12.564 --> 00:16:15.404
So, you know, if I had to pick
out of all of those habits

00:16:15.404 --> 00:16:17.134
I would, I would probably
pick making the bed

00:16:17.134 --> 00:16:19.344
and meditating if I had
to do only two things

00:16:19.344 --> 00:16:20.764
and meditating start five minutes.

00:16:20.764 --> 00:16:23.414
And literally there's
nothing mysterious about it.

00:16:23.414 --> 00:16:26.537
You just sit, you know, I try
to sit upright in a chair,

00:16:26.537 --> 00:16:30.464
hands on my lap and, you
know, kind of close my eyes

00:16:31.854 --> 00:16:34.477
Take some deep breaths in and out.

00:16:35.384 --> 00:16:36.514
In fact, do that right now.

00:16:36.514 --> 00:16:40.807
Just take some deep breaths in and out.

00:16:43.024 --> 00:16:45.944
And then I just start, you
know, focusing on my breaths

00:16:45.944 --> 00:16:49.884
and I try to steal my
mind as much as possible.

00:16:49.884 --> 00:16:52.024
Actually I was, I was
going there just now.

00:16:52.024 --> 00:16:53.392
So, but anyway, I, that's what I do.

00:16:53.392 --> 00:16:55.784
Maybe one day we'll do a meditation over

00:16:56.724 --> 00:16:58.924
this live stream, but I encourage
you to, you know, you see

00:16:58.924 --> 00:17:01.114
even those deep breaths, you
know, taking the deepest breath

00:17:01.114 --> 00:17:03.124
of your, of your week or your month

00:17:03.124 --> 00:17:06.501
in (inhaling) and out (exhaling).

00:17:07.574 --> 00:17:09.324
You'd be surprised what that does for you.

00:17:09.324 --> 00:17:11.014
And there's some good biology behind it.

00:17:11.014 --> 00:17:12.973
It activates your parasympathetic system

00:17:12.973 --> 00:17:15.204
which is the opposite of
your sympathetic system

00:17:15.204 --> 00:17:17.350
which is your fight or flight response.

00:17:17.350 --> 00:17:20.424
So it, it forces your body to relax.

00:17:20.424 --> 00:17:22.864
And then you still, your
mind then everything

00:17:22.864 --> 00:17:24.814
all of a sudden feels a lot less heavy.

00:17:25.734 --> 00:17:29.841
Let's see YouTube, Jason Suhei says,

00:17:29.841 --> 00:17:32.824
"Sal, I have learned many
subjects in my young life

00:17:32.824 --> 00:17:35.284
and have trouble retaining
that knowledge over time.

00:17:35.284 --> 00:17:36.774
How do you retain the vast amount

00:17:36.774 --> 00:17:40.164
of information that you've
procured over the years?"

00:17:40.164 --> 00:17:42.514
So I would say there's two

00:17:42.514 --> 00:17:46.594
or three ways to really retain knowledge.

00:17:46.594 --> 00:17:48.354
One is is while you're learning

00:17:48.354 --> 00:17:49.354
try to make as many connections

00:17:49.354 --> 00:17:50.304
as you can.

00:17:50.304 --> 00:17:51.664
Say okay, yeah, that's what I do

00:17:51.664 --> 00:17:53.404
for the test, but let me
draw some more connections.

00:17:53.404 --> 00:17:55.284
Let me derive that formula.

00:17:55.284 --> 00:17:57.244
Okay, this formula in
physics class says this

00:17:57.244 --> 00:17:58.814
but I think I saw something very similar

00:17:58.814 --> 00:18:00.854
in my calculus class.

00:18:00.854 --> 00:18:03.574
Or, hey, they're talking about
entropy and computer science

00:18:03.574 --> 00:18:06.374
and they're also talking
about entropy in a chemistry.

00:18:06.374 --> 00:18:07.534
How are these things related?

00:18:07.534 --> 00:18:09.495
And the more that you ponder these things

00:18:09.495 --> 00:18:12.184
the more that those
connections will be there.

00:18:12.184 --> 00:18:17.054
The second thing I'd recommend
is just applying them

00:18:17.054 --> 00:18:19.034
and you know, the, the
best way to apply a lot

00:18:19.034 --> 00:18:21.454
of these skills is actually
to teach it to other people.

00:18:21.454 --> 00:18:22.744
So if you teach

00:18:22.744 --> 00:18:25.384
if you make a habit of,
after you take a course

00:18:25.384 --> 00:18:26.804
the students who take it the next year

00:18:26.804 --> 00:18:28.974
you want to teach that course, trust me

00:18:28.974 --> 00:18:30.964
not only you're going to
learn it much, much deeper

00:18:30.964 --> 00:18:32.624
but you're going to
retain that information

00:18:32.624 --> 00:18:33.564
much much better.

00:18:33.564 --> 00:18:34.544
Some of you all know I have the

00:18:34.544 --> 00:18:36.314
side project schoolhouse.world,

00:18:36.314 --> 00:18:37.664
separate effort from Khan Academy,

00:18:37.664 --> 00:18:39.284
but it is a place where you can do that.

00:18:39.284 --> 00:18:41.784
Where if you need tutoring,
you can get free tutoring.

00:18:41.784 --> 00:18:43.414
But if you want to go deeper

00:18:43.414 --> 00:18:45.954
and your subject matter, you can go tutor.

00:18:45.954 --> 00:18:47.074
And actually we had an announcement with

00:18:47.074 --> 00:18:48.764
University of Chicago recently

00:18:48.764 --> 00:18:50.384
that they are going to be looking

00:18:50.384 --> 00:18:51.766
at students who are really good tutors

00:18:51.766 --> 00:18:55.714
of a subject because they see
if you can tutor the subject

00:18:55.714 --> 00:18:57.074
not only do you know the subject

00:18:57.074 --> 00:18:58.784
but that means you have
great communication

00:18:58.784 --> 00:18:59.617
you have empathy.

00:18:59.617 --> 00:19:01.461
And frankly, you're probably
going to retain that subject.

00:19:01.461 --> 00:19:03.434
And I think you're going to see a more

00:19:03.434 --> 00:19:04.574
and more of that in the future.

00:19:04.574 --> 00:19:06.628
So I highly recommend you to start

00:19:06.628 --> 00:19:10.264
tutoring the subject
yourself and using it.

00:19:10.264 --> 00:19:11.264
And then the third thing is

00:19:11.264 --> 00:19:12.574
you know, if you can, if you can keep

00:19:12.574 --> 00:19:13.824
revisiting the subject either

00:19:13.824 --> 00:19:15.884
because you're tutoring it or because

00:19:15.884 --> 00:19:17.094
you know, you just continue to stay

00:19:17.094 --> 00:19:19.624
curious that will also reinforce it,

00:19:19.624 --> 00:19:22.127
so the idea of staying in your head.

00:19:23.274 --> 00:19:24.607
So let's see.

00:19:25.724 --> 00:19:29.201
From YouTube Fatay Sidesal says.

00:19:29.201 --> 00:19:31.474
"Your English is very fluent.

00:19:31.474 --> 00:19:33.544
What tips do you recommend
to improve my English?"

00:19:33.544 --> 00:19:35.144
Well I can't take too much credit

00:19:35.144 --> 00:19:36.582
for my English being fluent

00:19:36.582 --> 00:19:39.454
because I was born in, in the U.S.

00:19:39.454 --> 00:19:41.104
in Metairie, Louisiana.

00:19:41.104 --> 00:19:43.254
And actually many people are surprised

00:19:43.254 --> 00:19:44.974
that I have what they perceive

00:19:44.974 --> 00:19:47.604
as a very neutral American accent

00:19:47.604 --> 00:19:48.804
despite the fact that I grew

00:19:48.804 --> 00:19:51.634
up in Southeast Louisiana,
but people don't realize

00:19:51.634 --> 00:19:56.254
that Metairie, Louisiana has
kind of a neutralish accent.

00:19:56.254 --> 00:19:57.685
Although there is a little bit of a

00:19:57.685 --> 00:20:01.374
it's called a YAT accent
that you kind of hear there.

00:20:01.374 --> 00:20:02.954
You'll hear my y'all every now

00:20:02.954 --> 00:20:05.624
and then, but that's why
I have very fluid English.

00:20:05.624 --> 00:20:07.754
So I can't take much credit for that.

00:20:07.754 --> 00:20:10.382
Other languages that I
kind of know, my Bengali

00:20:10.382 --> 00:20:15.382
and my Hindi/Urdu is not very fluent.

00:20:16.984 --> 00:20:18.781
It is (speaking foreign language) accent.

00:20:24.144 --> 00:20:28.542
That's my Hindi/Urdu and my Bengali.

00:20:28.542 --> 00:20:32.209
(speaking foreign language)

00:20:35.594 --> 00:20:36.964
That was my Bengali.

00:20:36.964 --> 00:20:38.604
So anyway, I just telling you know

00:20:38.604 --> 00:20:40.324
that it's always a journey
when you're not looking

00:20:40.324 --> 00:20:41.537
at your native language.

00:20:42.544 --> 00:20:47.184
Actually, those are my languages
where my family grew up,

00:20:47.184 --> 00:20:49.064
but those aren't the ones that I grew up.

00:20:49.064 --> 00:20:51.974
But my advice for you, if you know

00:20:51.974 --> 00:20:54.377
my theory, and I'm not
a linguistics expert

00:20:54.377 --> 00:20:57.384
is try to find people you
can have conversations with.

00:20:57.384 --> 00:20:58.264
And the beauty

00:20:58.264 --> 00:21:00.454
of the internet now is you don't have to

00:21:00.454 --> 00:21:04.494
necessarily travel to meet
people who are fluent,

00:21:04.494 --> 00:21:06.674
who are native speakers in
whatever language you want.

00:21:06.674 --> 00:21:08.684
So I would recommend if
you want to become fluent

00:21:08.684 --> 00:21:10.254
in English, I've got to believe,

00:21:10.254 --> 00:21:11.814
in fact I'm 100% sure there's

00:21:11.814 --> 00:21:14.774
places on the internet where
you can practice your English

00:21:14.774 --> 00:21:16.604
with native English speakers.

00:21:16.604 --> 00:21:17.834
And I think if you do a lot

00:21:17.834 --> 00:21:20.145
of that, not only will
your English improve

00:21:20.145 --> 00:21:22.694
but you'll probably meet
some interesting folks

00:21:22.694 --> 00:21:24.594
and they'll have a chance to meet you.

00:21:25.664 --> 00:21:26.624
So let's see

00:21:26.624 --> 00:21:28.061
from YouTube, Andrew Briggs says,

00:21:28.061 --> 00:21:30.904
"Do you have anything about
religion on Khan Academy?"

00:21:30.904 --> 00:21:33.024
We actually have a good bit about religion

00:21:33.024 --> 00:21:36.574
on Khan Academy from a
historical cultural lens.

00:21:36.574 --> 00:21:39.134
Especially if you look at
our world history content,

00:21:39.134 --> 00:21:40.004
we have a lot

00:21:40.004 --> 00:21:45.004
on kind of the Eastern traditions
on Hinduism on Buddhism.

00:21:46.394 --> 00:21:48.374
We have a lot on what I would say,

00:21:48.374 --> 00:21:51.101
you know, the more, I guess
you say Western religions,

00:21:51.101 --> 00:21:55.054
I guess mid Eastern religions is the rest.

00:21:55.054 --> 00:21:57.007
You know, so we have a lot

00:21:57.007 --> 00:21:59.834
we have a reasonable bit on
the core of Judaism, you know

00:21:59.834 --> 00:22:02.414
at least understanding the
narrative of the old Testament.

00:22:02.414 --> 00:22:03.774
We have a good bit

00:22:03.774 --> 00:22:07.964
about the, at least the
gospels, the kind of the life

00:22:07.964 --> 00:22:12.094
of Jesus and how Christianity
evolved from the time of Jesus

00:22:12.094 --> 00:22:15.614
under the Roman empire, all
the way through Constantine

00:22:15.614 --> 00:22:18.194
when the Roman empire, you
know, they transitioned

00:22:18.194 --> 00:22:20.684
from persecuting
Christians to becoming the

00:22:20.684 --> 00:22:24.184
you know, the kind of
the seat of Christianity.

00:22:24.184 --> 00:22:28.664
And we talk about, we have a
good about four or five videos

00:22:28.664 --> 00:22:30.784
on Islam where you can understand

00:22:30.784 --> 00:22:33.984
you know, the historical
underpinnings of it, how it started

00:22:33.984 --> 00:22:36.221
and how it grew historically.

00:22:36.221 --> 00:22:37.764
So that's what we have right now.

00:22:37.764 --> 00:22:38.784
I hope one day

00:22:38.784 --> 00:22:40.864
you know, we get a chance
to go deeper on all

00:22:40.864 --> 00:22:43.814
of these, because regardless
of whether you follow any

00:22:43.814 --> 00:22:47.384
of these religions or you
don't, whether you are religious

00:22:47.384 --> 00:22:50.862
whether you're atheist,
whatever your belief system is

00:22:50.862 --> 00:22:55.854
it's really valuable to
understand these religions

00:22:55.854 --> 00:22:56.744
which are, you know

00:22:56.744 --> 00:22:59.974
formed the base of so much of
our thought and our culture.

00:22:59.974 --> 00:23:02.430
So I highly recommend you're
taking a look at those things

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:05.280
on Khan Academy, if you're
not familiar with any of them.

00:23:06.244 --> 00:23:09.017
So let's see other questions.

00:23:09.954 --> 00:23:12.177
Let's see, I'm scrolling.

00:23:13.634 --> 00:23:16.824
There's a question here from YouTube.

00:23:16.824 --> 00:23:21.054
Mr. Specter asks, "Is your job stressful?

00:23:21.054 --> 00:23:23.034
How do you make all
these educational videos?

00:23:23.034 --> 00:23:24.324
How do you manage to make this

00:23:24.324 --> 00:23:26.924
nonprofit organization bloom?"

00:23:26.924 --> 00:23:28.584
So it's a good question, Mr. Specter.

00:23:28.584 --> 00:23:31.144
I'll tell you, there are
times that it is stressful.

00:23:31.144 --> 00:23:33.664
I'll tell you the video
making is not usually

00:23:33.664 --> 00:23:35.214
my source of stress.

00:23:35.214 --> 00:23:37.484
Sometimes it's a little
frustrating cause you know

00:23:37.484 --> 00:23:39.287
I keep bumbling over myself

00:23:39.287 --> 00:23:42.524
or I really want to make sure
I understand something really

00:23:42.524 --> 00:23:45.054
really well so I can get a
little bit compulsive about that.

00:23:45.054 --> 00:23:46.441
But for the most part, I find the

00:23:46.441 --> 00:23:48.744
video creation side to be almost

00:23:48.744 --> 00:23:51.274
the relaxing side of my job.

00:23:51.274 --> 00:23:53.764
It allows me to kind of, that's
where I get into my flow,

00:23:53.764 --> 00:23:55.344
when I get into my Zen.

00:23:55.344 --> 00:23:56.954
I think the stuff that gets stressful

00:23:56.954 --> 00:24:00.214
and you know, is there

00:24:00.214 --> 00:24:02.734
there's a lot of folks who
are dependent on Khan Academy.

00:24:02.734 --> 00:24:04.935
There's, you know, that
we have over 200 employees

00:24:04.935 --> 00:24:07.992
there's thousands of
volunteers around the world.

00:24:07.992 --> 00:24:10.974
We have hundreds of thousands of donors.

00:24:10.974 --> 00:24:13.594
We have tens of millions, if not hundreds

00:24:13.594 --> 00:24:14.954
of millions of users.

00:24:14.954 --> 00:24:18.294
And so, yeah, when, when
people are dependent on it

00:24:18.294 --> 00:24:22.874
you don't want to disappoint
folks and you can imagine

00:24:22.874 --> 00:24:26.293
you know, raising money, if
I don't raise enough money

00:24:26.293 --> 00:24:29.064
then we're not going to have
the resources to serve tens

00:24:29.064 --> 00:24:32.054
of millions of folks, or
we're not able to hire folks.

00:24:32.054 --> 00:24:36.704
Or, you know, even in the past
we've had to downsize some

00:24:36.704 --> 00:24:39.844
at Khan Academy, which is
an incredibly painful thing.

00:24:39.844 --> 00:24:42.639
So those types of things
develop a lot of stress.

00:24:42.639 --> 00:24:45.584
I would say there's there's
times also, when, you know

00:24:45.584 --> 00:24:47.144
you have passionate people who are

00:24:47.144 --> 00:24:49.177
trying to do the right thing.

00:24:49.177 --> 00:24:51.424
The root of passion is worth suffering.

00:24:51.424 --> 00:24:53.434
When you really care
about something, you know

00:24:53.434 --> 00:24:55.484
we can have heated debates
within Khan Academy

00:24:55.484 --> 00:24:57.782
of, you know, with the
scarce resources we have

00:24:57.782 --> 00:24:59.974
what is the best way to deploy that?

00:24:59.974 --> 00:25:02.334
Or what is the best
way to execute on that?

00:25:02.334 --> 00:25:03.934
And so, you know, I often do think

00:25:03.934 --> 00:25:07.664
about, you know, am I
supporting our team properly?

00:25:07.664 --> 00:25:08.694
Are we aligned?

00:25:08.694 --> 00:25:09.527
Are we working

00:25:09.527 --> 00:25:10.754
on the right things.

00:25:10.754 --> 00:25:13.474
You know, are the funders satisfied?

00:25:13.474 --> 00:25:15.844
You know, am I communicating
with them enough?

00:25:15.844 --> 00:25:18.614
So yeah, these, these can
sometimes make you wake

00:25:18.614 --> 00:25:19.447
up in the middle of the night

00:25:19.447 --> 00:25:22.314
but that's why I meditate
and take cold showers

00:25:22.314 --> 00:25:25.275
and do my push-ups and
pull-ups, and make my bed.

00:25:25.275 --> 00:25:27.321
And I make videos too that all,

00:25:27.321 --> 00:25:30.124
and I don't want to make it
sound like my whole, you know

00:25:30.124 --> 00:25:33.404
my job is like, you know,
some super stressful thing.

00:25:33.404 --> 00:25:34.374
It comes and goes, you know.

00:25:34.374 --> 00:25:37.884
There's moments of real joy.

00:25:37.884 --> 00:25:40.034
In fact it's mostly that,

00:25:40.034 --> 00:25:41.864
but yeah, there are definitely moments

00:25:41.864 --> 00:25:45.981
of stress and that how I deal with it.

00:25:47.894 --> 00:25:49.622
I will say one thing, you know, I remember

00:25:49.622 --> 00:25:52.824
when I used to read, I
don't, I would read, like

00:25:52.824 --> 00:25:54.194
I would watch like movies and there'd be

00:25:54.194 --> 00:25:56.444
like someone who runs a company
and they'd always be like

00:25:56.444 --> 00:25:58.014
Oh, it's so lonely at the top.

00:25:58.014 --> 00:25:59.874
And I'd be like, yeah,
it seems like it would

00:25:59.874 --> 00:26:02.184
be so awesome to be the boss.

00:26:02.184 --> 00:26:04.154
'Cause then you get to, you
know, whatever, you're the boss.

00:26:04.154 --> 00:26:07.354
How awesome is. I have to
say and not to feel sorry.

00:26:07.354 --> 00:26:09.744
I mean, cause I feel very privileged

00:26:09.744 --> 00:26:12.094
and lucky to have the role that
I have, but yeah, sometimes

00:26:12.094 --> 00:26:14.394
sometimes the stress is, 'cause you know

00:26:14.394 --> 00:26:17.314
you really can't tell other
folks everything that's going on

00:26:17.314 --> 00:26:18.318
in your head.

00:26:18.318 --> 00:26:20.601
And so you have to figure out ways to

00:26:20.601 --> 00:26:22.972
resolve your own head sometimes.

00:26:22.972 --> 00:26:26.267
But yeah, those are all the stresses.

00:26:27.274 --> 00:26:28.964
See other questions here.

00:26:28.964 --> 00:26:30.674
So someone's, so I use, she's asking

00:26:30.674 --> 00:26:34.194
for the photo explanation
of the photoelectric effect.

00:26:34.194 --> 00:26:37.534
I'm tempted, but we have
videos on that on Khan Academy.

00:26:37.534 --> 00:26:40.614
So I encourage you to go there

00:26:40.614 --> 00:26:41.894
and if that's not satisfying, let us know.

00:26:41.894 --> 00:26:43.704
And we'll, I'll make another video

00:26:43.704 --> 00:26:47.084
to answer any other
questions you might have.

00:26:47.084 --> 00:26:51.071
Let's see from Facebook,
Victoria Angela Umedo says,

00:26:51.071 --> 00:26:52.574
"I'm working on getting a GED,

00:26:52.574 --> 00:26:54.814
but I get overwhelmed,
worried and stressed.

00:26:54.814 --> 00:26:55.647
Any advice?

00:26:55.647 --> 00:26:56.734
Thank you."

00:26:56.734 --> 00:26:58.204
Well, my advice is, you know

00:26:58.204 --> 00:27:00.355
if anyone's getting overwhelmed
or stressed about anything

00:27:00.355 --> 00:27:04.007
one, you can't underestimate the power

00:27:04.007 --> 00:27:06.574
or you can't overestimate the power

00:27:06.574 --> 00:27:09.254
of meditation even five, 10 minutes a day.

00:27:09.254 --> 00:27:10.886
Once again, this is nothing mystical.

00:27:10.886 --> 00:27:14.344
It's just you being able
to observe your thoughts

00:27:14.344 --> 00:27:16.734
recognize that you are not your thoughts.

00:27:16.734 --> 00:27:19.151
You are not those negative
feelings that you are.

00:27:19.151 --> 00:27:21.444
You know, those feelings
are real they're happening

00:27:21.444 --> 00:27:24.598
inside your brain, but you
are something more than that.

00:27:24.598 --> 00:27:25.431
And that, you know

00:27:25.431 --> 00:27:28.144
when you start to observe,
your mind starts to still

00:27:28.144 --> 00:27:30.814
and you do literally
feel kind of a space form

00:27:30.814 --> 00:27:32.704
and you can see, okay, there's
this thought that saying

00:27:32.704 --> 00:27:34.524
oh, I'm not going to be
able to achieve my goal

00:27:34.524 --> 00:27:36.824
or what are people gonna
think of me and all of that.

00:27:36.824 --> 00:27:39.244
But you're like, well, that's
just a thought that's not me.

00:27:39.244 --> 00:27:40.771
And then slowly that kind of can die down.

00:27:40.771 --> 00:27:43.254
And that I find once I get

00:27:43.254 --> 00:27:45.964
to that point, I'm just like,
you know what, regardless

00:27:45.964 --> 00:27:49.644
of the outcome, let me just
do what's the right next step.

00:27:49.644 --> 00:27:53.364
And if I just do the right
next step, I'll make progress.

00:27:53.364 --> 00:27:56.034
Hopefully, and the chips
fall where they do, you know

00:27:56.034 --> 00:27:57.214
yesterday, our guests

00:27:57.214 --> 00:28:00.224
of Vavek, he talked about the
former surgeon general talked

00:28:00.224 --> 00:28:01.454
about are reacting with hope.

00:28:01.454 --> 00:28:04.204
Are we acting with love or
are we acting with fear?

00:28:04.204 --> 00:28:06.654
And I think, you know, this feelings

00:28:06.654 --> 00:28:09.124
of procrastination feeling overwhelmed,

00:28:09.124 --> 00:28:10.294
these are feelings of fear.

00:28:10.294 --> 00:28:11.914
You're like, Oh, well, what if I do this

00:28:11.914 --> 00:28:12.914
and it doesn't work out.

00:28:12.914 --> 00:28:14.874
And you know, people
are going to think this

00:28:14.874 --> 00:28:16.274
about me and that's going to happen.

00:28:16.274 --> 00:28:18.284
I'm not going to get that opportunity.

00:28:18.284 --> 00:28:20.200
Instead, I would say, try to act

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:23.664
out of love or hope or however
you want to describe it.

00:28:23.664 --> 00:28:24.564
We say, well, you know what?

00:28:24.564 --> 00:28:25.944
I'm just going to do the right thing

00:28:25.944 --> 00:28:28.284
at any step of the way,
put one foot in front

00:28:28.284 --> 00:28:31.034
of the other, try to make
progress, be a little bit further

00:28:31.034 --> 00:28:33.714
along today than I was yesterday,
a little bit further along

00:28:33.714 --> 00:28:35.386
in the next hour than I
was in the previous hour.

00:28:35.386 --> 00:28:38.474
And that you're going to notice
that you're making progress.

00:28:38.474 --> 00:28:40.054
I mean, it's like climbing a big mountain.

00:28:40.054 --> 00:28:41.184
You just put one foot in front

00:28:41.184 --> 00:28:43.614
of the other and you know,
before you know it, you look back

00:28:43.614 --> 00:28:45.654
and you've, you've actually
made a lot of progress.

00:28:45.654 --> 00:28:48.334
So that'd be my, my meta advice.

00:28:48.334 --> 00:28:51.764
I would say, just make a
habit of doing the practice.

00:28:51.764 --> 00:28:52.888
When you get things wrong

00:28:52.888 --> 00:28:55.674
don't beat up yourself, remind yourself.

00:28:55.674 --> 00:28:56.634
That's actually moments

00:28:56.634 --> 00:28:59.984
of growth, reflect on why you
got it wrong and keep going.

00:28:59.984 --> 00:29:03.664
You know, I, I'm a amateur
guitarist and you know

00:29:03.664 --> 00:29:06.044
there are these guitar chords that

00:29:06.044 --> 00:29:08.654
you know, I've never been a fan

00:29:08.654 --> 00:29:10.854
of, but lately I've been
like, you know what?

00:29:10.854 --> 00:29:13.174
I needed to just keep
practicing them everyday.

00:29:13.174 --> 00:29:15.041
And it is amazing that your

00:29:15.041 --> 00:29:19.104
your brain slowly, just these
things that felt impossible

00:29:19.104 --> 00:29:21.284
for you after a week or two

00:29:21.284 --> 00:29:24.674
all of a sudden feel very
natural and very automatic.

00:29:24.674 --> 00:29:28.064
So I would just say, keep
at it, keep a habit of it.

00:29:28.064 --> 00:29:31.944
And you know, and always keep perspective.

00:29:31.944 --> 00:29:33.204
Let's see, Oh, we're all out of time.

00:29:33.204 --> 00:29:36.534
This goes, this goes by
very, very, very fast.

00:29:36.534 --> 00:29:39.038
So I will leave y'all there.

00:29:39.038 --> 00:29:41.104
I'll give one final announcement.

00:29:41.104 --> 00:29:43.264
Next week I'm sure many people

00:29:43.264 --> 00:29:46.174
if you're 18 or over, have
already voted through your mail

00:29:46.174 --> 00:29:49.824
in ballots, but remember on
Tuesday it's election day.

00:29:49.824 --> 00:29:51.994
So definitely vote.

00:29:51.994 --> 00:29:55.014
We're only going to have
one live stream next week

00:29:55.014 --> 00:29:56.022
on Wednesday, we're going to have former

00:29:56.022 --> 00:30:00.394
Secretary of Education,
Margaret Spellings.

00:30:00.394 --> 00:30:02.434
And I think there's going to
be a very similar conversation

00:30:02.434 --> 00:30:05.654
to what we had a conversation
with RD Dunkin last week

00:30:05.654 --> 00:30:08.034
but really just about,
you know, what's going

00:30:08.034 --> 00:30:11.234
on in education and how we
can best navigate the kind

00:30:11.234 --> 00:30:14.624
of the education crisis
due to COVID together.

00:30:14.624 --> 00:30:15.944
So look forward to seeing all

00:30:15.944 --> 00:30:20.424
of y'all next Wednesday at
12 Pacific, three Eastern.

00:30:20.424 --> 00:30:21.636
See you then.

00:30:21.636 --> 00:30:24.219
(logo chiming)

